INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$9e3a215b$SATQ$, $SATQ$de611823-9021-492c-83a0-18ecca3bd27c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A 2022 study by researchers Hala Altamimi and Qiaozhen Liu investigated the relationship between nonprofit arts organizations&rsquo; spending and performance. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the researchers examined the correlation between how much 22,328 US arts nonprofits spent on overhead&mdash;operational costs such as equipment and fundraising&mdash;and how many people attended their events (a measure of overall success).</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f75e3c50-276f-46a9-b878-cd566f6579fa","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"},{"id":"2cdae2ef-2aa9-48da-a9da-ea07abaa194f","content":"<p>In addition,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"3f62fa2f-5a5d-4e2d-b769-7e62b357719f","content":"<p>By comparison,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"cbfcac9b-5def-43a2-9083-da5c699216d2","content":"<p>Specifically,&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["cbfcac9b-5def-43a2-9083-da5c699216d2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "Specifically" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that the researchers examined the overhead costs and attendance at events of 22,328 nonprofits&mdash;provides specific, precise details elaborating on how the researchers investigated the relationship between nonprofits&rsquo; spending and performance.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. "Thus" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a result or consequence of the researchers&rsquo; investigation of nonprofits&rsquo; spending and performance. Instead, it specifies how they examined that correlation. Choice B is incorrect. "In addition" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is merely an additional fact about the researchers&rsquo; investigation of nonprofits&rsquo; spending and performance. Instead, it specifies how they examined that correlation. Choice C is incorrect. "By comparison" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is being compared to the researchers&rsquo; investigation of nonprofits&rsquo; spending and performance. Instead, it specifies how they examined that correlation.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9e3a215b","external_id":"de611823-9021-492c-83a0-18ecca3bd27c","vaultid":null,"uId":"b3e2436d-cedb-4425-ac76-86f99ce007bb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A 2022 study by researchers Hala Altamimi and Qiaozhen Liu investigated the relationship between nonprofit arts organizations&rsquo; spending and performance. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the researchers examined the correlation between how much 22,328 US arts nonprofits spent on overhead&mdash;operational costs such as equipment and fundraising&mdash;and how many people attended their events (a measure of overall success).</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f75e3c50-276f-46a9-b878-cd566f6579fa","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"},{"id":"2cdae2ef-2aa9-48da-a9da-ea07abaa194f","content":"<p>In addition,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"3f62fa2f-5a5d-4e2d-b769-7e62b357719f","content":"<p>By comparison,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"cbfcac9b-5def-43a2-9083-da5c699216d2","content":"<p>Specifically,&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["cbfcac9b-5def-43a2-9083-da5c699216d2"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"Specifically\" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that the researchers examined the overhead costs and attendance at events of 22,328 nonprofits&mdash;provides specific, precise details elaborating on how the researchers investigated the relationship between nonprofits&rsquo; spending and performance.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. \"Thus\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a result or consequence of the researchers&rsquo; investigation of nonprofits&rsquo; spending and performance. Instead, it specifies how they examined that correlation. Choice B is incorrect. \"In addition\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is merely an additional fact about the researchers&rsquo; investigation of nonprofits&rsquo; spending and performance. Instead, it specifies how they examined that correlation. Choice C is incorrect. \"By comparison\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is being compared to the researchers&rsquo; investigation of nonprofits&rsquo; spending and performance. Instead, it specifies how they examined that correlation.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323044,"updateDate":1730147323044}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323044, 1730147323044),
    ($SATQ$9f1a0d91$SATQ$, $SATQ$2c4d4984-16b3-4f55-ae49-6d5fa79b99cf$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$83e2ce96-4e51-4b02-b018-d56bcdfabd19$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>&ldquo;Tulip mania&rdquo;&mdash;the rapid rise and sudden fall of the price of tulip bulbs in seventeenth-century Amsterdam&mdash;is often cited as an example of the perils of rampant market speculation. However, recent research has demonstrated that the episode was neither as frenzied nor as disastrous as has been thought. The popular myth surrounding it, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> should be regarded with some skepticism.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"470d72d8-552e-4f56-8764-1e8767d6e96f","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"e4d97c24-139c-4161-a35f-15ea75aabf93","content":"<p>by contrast,</p>"},{"id":"10dfc39f-cfc9-43ec-a63d-4c787bb46d97","content":"<p>nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"86864ed1-5112-4729-863a-e2a6caaa9e86","content":"<p>therefore,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["86864ed1-5112-4729-863a-e2a6caaa9e86"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. This sentence is arguing that new evidence contradicting popular beliefs about &ldquo;tulip mania&rdquo; should cast doubt on those beliefs. &ldquo;Therefore&rdquo; is a cause-and-effect transition, which fits perfectly in this context. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses an exemplification transition, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. Skepticism about the popular beliefs is not an example of recent evidence contradicting those beliefs&mdash;rather, skepticism is an effect of that recent evidence. Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But this sentence doesn&rsquo;t disagree with the previous sentence. Instead, it connects a cause from the previous sentence (new evidence that tulip mania was not as disastrous as thought) to an effect (that we should look with skepticism upon the myth about its disastrousness). Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But this sentence doesn&rsquo;t disagree with the previous sentence. Instead, it connects a cause from the previous sentence (new evidence that tulip mania was not as disastrous as thought) to an effect (that we should look with skepticism upon the myth about its disastrousness). </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TRA-H008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$a081dd1b-d9dc-4e49-8e5a-5cca1d9b6f23$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9f1a0d91","external_id":"2c4d4984-16b3-4f55-ae49-6d5fa79b99cf","vaultid":"83e2ce96-4e51-4b02-b018-d56bcdfabd19","uId":"8a662040-84c1-47ff-8a12-4b0f6a51808d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>&ldquo;Tulip mania&rdquo;&mdash;the rapid rise and sudden fall of the price of tulip bulbs in seventeenth-century Amsterdam&mdash;is often cited as an example of the perils of rampant market speculation. However, recent research has demonstrated that the episode was neither as frenzied nor as disastrous as has been thought. The popular myth surrounding it, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> should be regarded with some skepticism.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"470d72d8-552e-4f56-8764-1e8767d6e96f","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"e4d97c24-139c-4161-a35f-15ea75aabf93","content":"<p>by contrast,</p>"},{"id":"10dfc39f-cfc9-43ec-a63d-4c787bb46d97","content":"<p>nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"86864ed1-5112-4729-863a-e2a6caaa9e86","content":"<p>therefore,</p>"}],"keys":["86864ed1-5112-4729-863a-e2a6caaa9e86"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. This sentence is arguing that new evidence contradicting popular beliefs about &ldquo;tulip mania&rdquo; should cast doubt on those beliefs. &ldquo;Therefore&rdquo; is a cause-and-effect transition, which fits perfectly in this context. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses an exemplification transition, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. Skepticism about the popular beliefs is not an example of recent evidence contradicting those beliefs&mdash;rather, skepticism is an effect of that recent evidence. Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But this sentence doesn&rsquo;t disagree with the previous sentence. Instead, it connects a cause from the previous sentence (new evidence that tulip mania was not as disastrous as thought) to an effect (that we should look with skepticism upon the myth about its disastrousness). Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But this sentence doesn&rsquo;t disagree with the previous sentence. Instead, it connects a cause from the previous sentence (new evidence that tulip mania was not as disastrous as thought) to an effect (that we should look with skepticism upon the myth about its disastrousness). </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TRA-H008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"a081dd1b-d9dc-4e49-8e5a-5cca1d9b6f23","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959373,"updateDate":1691007959373}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959373, 1691007959373),
    ($SATQ$9fe7315b$SATQ$, $SATQ$d855d8ce-4083-44c4-b28d-ed0e0aaa7543$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Few New Yorkers have heard Thelma Pollard&rsquo;s name. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> many have seen her work in the famous Broadway musical <em>The Phantom of the Opera</em>. As the musical&rsquo;s longtime makeup supervisor, Pollard put the iconic makeup on the face of the Phantom himself.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8c4af20e-e415-4891-89df-a3b9ee6de116","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"842242ce-1ea2-46f7-a1f3-6722084a7db4","content":"<p>Therefore,</p>"},{"id":"a1f23fc3-2149-42ee-aeef-7483f7022327","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"7cfc1400-c771-4064-b8d4-90d77f9dde5a","content":"<p>However,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7cfc1400-c771-4064-b8d4-90d77f9dde5a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "However" logically signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that many New Yorkers have seen Pollard&rsquo;s work in a famous Broadway musical&mdash;is true despite the previous information that few New Yorkers have heard Pollard&rsquo;s name.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "similarly" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is similar to the previous information that few New Yorkers have heard Pollard&rsquo;s name. Instead, the claim that many New Yorkers have seen Pollard&rsquo;s work is true despite the previous information. Choice B is incorrect because "therefore" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is a result of the previous information that few New Yorkers have heard Pollard&rsquo;s name. Instead, the claim that many New Yorkers have seen Pollard&rsquo;s work is true despite the previous information. Choice C is incorrect because "for example" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence exemplifies the previous information that few New Yorkers have heard Pollard&rsquo;s name. Instead, the claim that many New Yorkers have seen Pollard&rsquo;s work is true despite the previous information.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9fe7315b","external_id":"d855d8ce-4083-44c4-b28d-ed0e0aaa7543","vaultid":null,"uId":"5cb52936-7a79-415c-a862-2312f83f2c41","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Few New Yorkers have heard Thelma Pollard&rsquo;s name. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> many have seen her work in the famous Broadway musical <em>The Phantom of the Opera</em>. As the musical&rsquo;s longtime makeup supervisor, Pollard put the iconic makeup on the face of the Phantom himself.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8c4af20e-e415-4891-89df-a3b9ee6de116","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"842242ce-1ea2-46f7-a1f3-6722084a7db4","content":"<p>Therefore,</p>"},{"id":"a1f23fc3-2149-42ee-aeef-7483f7022327","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"7cfc1400-c771-4064-b8d4-90d77f9dde5a","content":"<p>However,</p>"}],"keys":["7cfc1400-c771-4064-b8d4-90d77f9dde5a"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"However\" logically signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that many New Yorkers have seen Pollard&rsquo;s work in a famous Broadway musical&mdash;is true despite the previous information that few New Yorkers have heard Pollard&rsquo;s name.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"similarly\" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is similar to the previous information that few New Yorkers have heard Pollard&rsquo;s name. Instead, the claim that many New Yorkers have seen Pollard&rsquo;s work is true despite the previous information. Choice B is incorrect because \"therefore\" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is a result of the previous information that few New Yorkers have heard Pollard&rsquo;s name. Instead, the claim that many New Yorkers have seen Pollard&rsquo;s work is true despite the previous information. Choice C is incorrect because \"for example\" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence exemplifies the previous information that few New Yorkers have heard Pollard&rsquo;s name. Instead, the claim that many New Yorkers have seen Pollard&rsquo;s work is true despite the previous information.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323044,"updateDate":1730147323044}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323044, 1730147323044),
    ($SATQ$a064955f$SATQ$, $SATQ$71f2c191-fd2c-4b76-ac17-97ec944fd8c4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In most deer species, males grow antlers, and females don&rsquo;t. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> reindeer are different. They are the only deer species in which the females grow antlers, too.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a9560c8c-7d11-4149-93b5-6e016d24ef04","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"27128767-b51d-4684-9626-434fe186022e","content":"<p>Next,</p>"},{"id":"3eb1f6b3-abdd-4f9c-8ed5-c35bddb14e54","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"5b4fbd15-d986-48b1-907d-81e47144e9ef","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3eb1f6b3-abdd-4f9c-8ed5-c35bddb14e54"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. "However" logically signals that the claim about reindeer in this sentence contrasts with the information about most deer species in the previous sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "similarly" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is similar to the previous information about most deer species. Instead, it contrasts with that information. Choice B is incorrect because "next" illogically signals that the claim about reindeer in this sentence is the next step in a process. Instead, it contrasts with the previous information about most deer species. Choice D is incorrect because "thus" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence results from the previous information about most deer species. Instead, it contrasts with that information.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a064955f","external_id":"71f2c191-fd2c-4b76-ac17-97ec944fd8c4","vaultid":null,"uId":"9a119264-86ba-4529-bfc4-b813c040de38","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In most deer species, males grow antlers, and females don&rsquo;t. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> reindeer are different. They are the only deer species in which the females grow antlers, too.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a9560c8c-7d11-4149-93b5-6e016d24ef04","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"27128767-b51d-4684-9626-434fe186022e","content":"<p>Next,</p>"},{"id":"3eb1f6b3-abdd-4f9c-8ed5-c35bddb14e54","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"5b4fbd15-d986-48b1-907d-81e47144e9ef","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"}],"keys":["3eb1f6b3-abdd-4f9c-8ed5-c35bddb14e54"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. \"However\" logically signals that the claim about reindeer in this sentence contrasts with the information about most deer species in the previous sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"similarly\" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is similar to the previous information about most deer species. Instead, it contrasts with that information. Choice B is incorrect because \"next\" illogically signals that the claim about reindeer in this sentence is the next step in a process. Instead, it contrasts with the previous information about most deer species. Choice D is incorrect because \"thus\" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence results from the previous information about most deer species. Instead, it contrasts with that information.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323043,"updateDate":1730147323043}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323043, 1730147323043),
    ($SATQ$a0da8114$SATQ$, $SATQ$1ee45bc4-2b6f-486e-aef5-02fd321f778a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Tibetan mastiffs are large dogs native to the Himalayas.</li>
<li>A mutation in their EPAS1 gene prevents excess hemoglobin production.</li>
<li>A mutation in their HBB gene boosts hemoglobin&rsquo;s oxygen-carrying ability.</li>
<li>These mutations enable the dogs to withstand hypoxic (low-oxygen) conditions at high altitudes.</li>
<li>In a 2016 study, Zhen Wang and colleagues noted that Tibetan wolves&rsquo; DNA has the same EPAS1 and HBB mutations. &nbsp;</li>
<li>Wang and colleagues determined that the dogs first acquired these mutations by interbreeding with Tibetan wolves around 24,000 years ago.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to present the conclusion of Zhen Wang and colleagues&rsquo; 2016 study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c9c4ddff-2b14-43bb-802f-bb8036d3561b","content":"<p>Like Tibetan mastiffs, Tibetan wolves can withstand hypoxic conditions at high altitudes.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"12f3172b-2443-4108-81e7-b58927c431f4","content":"<p>Both Tibetan mastiffs and Tibetan wolves have mutations in their EPAS1 and HBB genes, which prevent excess hemoglobin production and boost hemoglobin&rsquo;s oxygen-carrying ability, respectively.</p>"},{"id":"86b00e0e-4bde-4f07-8e53-1dc773797bea","content":"<p>In addition to preventing excess hemoglobin production, a mutation in Tibetan mastiffs&rsquo; HBB gene boosts hemoglobin&rsquo;s oxygen-carrying ability.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"5b2e7841-8882-44e7-896d-0634c97af859","content":"<p>By interbreeding with Tibetan wolves around 24,000 years ago, Tibetan mastiffs acquired the genetic mutations that enable them to withstand hypoxic conditions.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5b2e7841-8882-44e7-896d-0634c97af859"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence presents the conclusion of Zhen Wang and colleagues&rsquo; 2016 study: Tibetan mastiffs are able to withstand hypoxic conditions due to their interbreeding with Tibetan wolves 24,000 years ago (which allowed the mastiffs to acquire the necessary genetic mutations).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between Tibetan mastiffs and Tibetan wolves; it doesn&rsquo;t present the conclusions of the 2016 study. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the genes of Tibetan mastiffs and Tibetan wolves; it doesn&rsquo;t present the conclusions of the 2016 study. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents information from the notes by indicating that a mutation in mastiffs&rsquo; HBB gene prevents excess hemoglobin production; moreover, it doesn&rsquo;t present the conclusions of the 2016 study.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a0da8114","external_id":"1ee45bc4-2b6f-486e-aef5-02fd321f778a","vaultid":null,"uId":"77342224-5798-435f-8810-da36aabac5cc","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tibetan mastiffs are large dogs native to the Himalayas.</li>\n<li>A mutation in their EPAS1 gene prevents excess hemoglobin production.</li>\n<li>A mutation in their HBB gene boosts hemoglobin&rsquo;s oxygen-carrying ability.</li>\n<li>These mutations enable the dogs to withstand hypoxic (low-oxygen) conditions at high altitudes.</li>\n<li>In a 2016 study, Zhen Wang and colleagues noted that Tibetan wolves&rsquo; DNA has the same EPAS1 and HBB mutations. &nbsp;</li>\n<li>Wang and colleagues determined that the dogs first acquired these mutations by interbreeding with Tibetan wolves around 24,000 years ago.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to present the conclusion of Zhen Wang and colleagues&rsquo; 2016 study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c9c4ddff-2b14-43bb-802f-bb8036d3561b","content":"<p>Like Tibetan mastiffs, Tibetan wolves can withstand hypoxic conditions at high altitudes.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"12f3172b-2443-4108-81e7-b58927c431f4","content":"<p>Both Tibetan mastiffs and Tibetan wolves have mutations in their EPAS1 and HBB genes, which prevent excess hemoglobin production and boost hemoglobin&rsquo;s oxygen-carrying ability, respectively.</p>"},{"id":"86b00e0e-4bde-4f07-8e53-1dc773797bea","content":"<p>In addition to preventing excess hemoglobin production, a mutation in Tibetan mastiffs&rsquo; HBB gene boosts hemoglobin&rsquo;s oxygen-carrying ability.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"5b2e7841-8882-44e7-896d-0634c97af859","content":"<p>By interbreeding with Tibetan wolves around 24,000 years ago, Tibetan mastiffs acquired the genetic mutations that enable them to withstand hypoxic conditions.</p>"}],"keys":["5b2e7841-8882-44e7-896d-0634c97af859"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence presents the conclusion of Zhen Wang and colleagues&rsquo; 2016 study: Tibetan mastiffs are able to withstand hypoxic conditions due to their interbreeding with Tibetan wolves 24,000 years ago (which allowed the mastiffs to acquire the necessary genetic mutations).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between Tibetan mastiffs and Tibetan wolves; it doesn&rsquo;t present the conclusions of the 2016 study. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the genes of Tibetan mastiffs and Tibetan wolves; it doesn&rsquo;t present the conclusions of the 2016 study. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents information from the notes by indicating that a mutation in mastiffs&rsquo; HBB gene prevents excess hemoglobin production; moreover, it doesn&rsquo;t present the conclusions of the 2016 study.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669204,"updateDate":1743430669204}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669204, 1743430669204),
    ($SATQ$a1955620$SATQ$, $SATQ$6e5f7212-59e5-4e7d-ac52-76fabf8563bf$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Edmonia Lewis (1844&ndash;1907) was an African American and Mississauga Ojibwe sculptor.</li>
<li><em>Forever Free</em> (<span data-ssml-say-as="date">1867</span>) is a marble sculpture by Lewis.</li>
<li>It depicts a male figure and a female figure gazing upward.</li>
<li>It commemorates the <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1863</span> Emancipation Proclamation.</li>
<li>The phrase &ldquo;forever free&rdquo; from the text of the Emancipation Proclamation is inscribed on the sculpture&rsquo;s base.</li>
<li>Art historian Kirsten Buick describes the sculpture as a &ldquo;celebration of liberty.&rdquo;</li>
</ul></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to explain the sculpture&rsquo;s specific historical context. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"769be612-7a86-44a0-993b-4d6107f99c53","content":"<p>According to art historian Kirsten Buick, <em>Forever Free</em>, which depicts a male figure and a female figure gazing upward, is a &ldquo;celebration of liberty.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e46a54e1-b66b-4f5d-848f-9abe2e55b792","content":"<p>The base of Edmonia Lewis&rsquo;s <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1867</span> marble sculpture is inscribed with a historically significant phrase: &ldquo;forever free.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"22672aac-cedc-4af8-8e6f-f8c38833ce76","content":"<p>Completed in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1867</span>, Lewis&rsquo;s sculpture <em>Forever Free</em> commemorates the Emancipation Proclamation, which had been issued four years previously.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"8cb74fcb-7e61-42f8-941e-311144df166b","content":"<p><em>Forever Free</em> (<span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1867</span>) is a marble sculpture by Edmonia Lewis, an African American and Mississauga Ojibwe sculptor who lived from <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1844</span> to <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1907</span>.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["22672aac-cedc-4af8-8e6f-f8c38833ce76"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence explains the specific historical context of Lewis&rsquo;s sculpture, noting that it commemorates the Emancipation Proclamation, which was issued four years before the sculpture was completed in 1867.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence quotes an art historian&rsquo;s general interpretation of the sculpture&rsquo;s meaning; it doesn&rsquo;t explain the sculpture&rsquo;s specific historical context. Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence mentions a historically significant phrase on the sculpture&rsquo;s base, it doesn&rsquo;t explain the specific historical context of the phrase or the sculpture. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence provides biographical information about Lewis; it doesn&rsquo;t explain the specific historical context of the sculpture.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a1955620","external_id":"6e5f7212-59e5-4e7d-ac52-76fabf8563bf","vaultid":null,"uId":"4ac74d56-618a-4fc1-8dc9-02e189caab25","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Edmonia Lewis (1844&ndash;1907) was an African American and Mississauga Ojibwe sculptor.</li>\n<li><em>Forever Free</em> (<span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1867</span>) is a marble sculpture by Lewis.</li>\n<li>It depicts a male figure and a female figure gazing upward.</li>\n<li>It commemorates the <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1863</span> Emancipation Proclamation.</li>\n<li>The phrase &ldquo;forever free&rdquo; from the text of the Emancipation Proclamation is inscribed on the sculpture&rsquo;s base.</li>\n<li>Art historian Kirsten Buick describes the sculpture as a &ldquo;celebration of liberty.&rdquo;</li>\n</ul></p>","stem":"<p>The student wants to explain the sculpture&rsquo;s specific historical context. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"769be612-7a86-44a0-993b-4d6107f99c53","content":"<p>According to art historian Kirsten Buick, <em>Forever Free</em>, which depicts a male figure and a female figure gazing upward, is a &ldquo;celebration of liberty.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e46a54e1-b66b-4f5d-848f-9abe2e55b792","content":"<p>The base of Edmonia Lewis&rsquo;s <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1867</span> marble sculpture is inscribed with a historically significant phrase: &ldquo;forever free.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"22672aac-cedc-4af8-8e6f-f8c38833ce76","content":"<p>Completed in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1867</span>, Lewis&rsquo;s sculpture <em>Forever Free</em> commemorates the Emancipation Proclamation, which had been issued four years previously.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"8cb74fcb-7e61-42f8-941e-311144df166b","content":"<p><em>Forever Free</em> (<span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1867</span>) is a marble sculpture by Edmonia Lewis, an African American and Mississauga Ojibwe sculptor who lived from <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1844</span> to <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1907</span>.</p>"}],"keys":["22672aac-cedc-4af8-8e6f-f8c38833ce76"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence explains the specific historical context of Lewis&rsquo;s sculpture, noting that it commemorates the Emancipation Proclamation, which was issued four years before the sculpture was completed in 1867.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence quotes an art historian&rsquo;s general interpretation of the sculpture&rsquo;s meaning; it doesn&rsquo;t explain the sculpture&rsquo;s specific historical context. Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence mentions a historically significant phrase on the sculpture&rsquo;s base, it doesn&rsquo;t explain the specific historical context of the phrase or the sculpture. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence provides biographical information about Lewis; it doesn&rsquo;t explain the specific historical context of the sculpture.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$a1ca7ec4$SATQ$, $SATQ$b7e7eb1d-dcce-4ecd-9297-2114edf6be7d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Cecilia Vicu&ntilde;a is a multidisciplinary artist.</li>
<li>In 1971, her first solo art exhibition, <em>Pinturas, poemas y explicaciones</em>, was shown at the Museo Nacional de Bellas Artes in Santiago, Chile.</li>
<li>Her poetry collection <em>Precario/Precarious</em> was published in 1983 by Tanam Press.</li>
<li>Her poetry collection <em>Instan</em> was published in 2002 by Kelsey <span data-ssml-sub-alias="St">St.</span> Press.</li>
<li>She lives part time in Chile, where she was born, and part time in New York.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to introduce the artist&rsquo;s 1983 poetry collection. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"591cc87d-3841-4aec-8e8c-7f464096d09a","content":"<p>Before she published the books <em>Precario/Precarious</em> (1983) and <em>Instan </em>(2002), Cecilia Vicu&ntilde;a exhibited visual art at the Museo Nacional de Bellas Artes in Santiago, Chile.</p>"},{"id":"f33f5968-d2e2-4388-b885-84f221b52917","content":"<p>Cecilia Vicu&ntilde;a is a true multidisciplinary artist whose works include numerous poetry collections and visual art exhibitions.</p>"},{"id":"a3f5ca12-e88a-4c39-9379-cc3cadf47502","content":"<p>Published in 1983 by Tanam Press, <em>Precario/Precarious</em> is a collection of poetry by the multidisciplinary artist Cecilia Vicu&ntilde;a.</p>"},{"id":"d2ed9d21-39ff-48f1-b4d5-9808d8a1ccd1","content":"<p>In 1971, Cecilia Vicu&ntilde;a exhibited her first solo art exhibition, <em>Pinturas, poemas y explicaciones</em>, in Chile, her country of birth.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a3f5ca12-e88a-4c39-9379-cc3cadf47502"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence effectively introduces the poetry collection <em>Precario/Precarious</em>, noting that it is a collection by Vicu&ntilde;a that was published in 1983 by Tanam Press.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence mentions the 1983 poetry collection <em>Precario/Precarious</em>, it focuses mainly on Vicu&ntilde;a&rsquo;s visual art. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence doesn&rsquo;t introduce the 1983 poetry collection <em>Precario/Precarious</em>; instead, it introduces Vicu&ntilde;a. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the location of Vicu&ntilde;a&rsquo;s 1971 exhibition <em>Pinturas, poemas y explicaciones</em>; it doesn&rsquo;t introduce the 1983 poetry collection <em>Precario/Precarious</em><em>.</em></p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a1ca7ec4","external_id":"b7e7eb1d-dcce-4ecd-9297-2114edf6be7d","vaultid":null,"uId":"9803c2b4-7b6f-4d21-b4bf-12bba7681475","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cecilia Vicu&ntilde;a is a multidisciplinary artist.</li>\n<li>In 1971, her first solo art exhibition, <em>Pinturas, poemas y explicaciones</em>, was shown at the Museo Nacional de Bellas Artes in Santiago, Chile.</li>\n<li>Her poetry collection <em>Precario/Precarious</em> was published in 1983 by Tanam Press.</li>\n<li>Her poetry collection <em>Instan</em> was published in 2002 by Kelsey <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"St\">St.</span> Press.</li>\n<li>She lives part time in Chile, where she was born, and part time in New York.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to introduce the artist&rsquo;s 1983 poetry collection. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"591cc87d-3841-4aec-8e8c-7f464096d09a","content":"<p>Before she published the books <em>Precario/Precarious</em> (1983) and <em>Instan </em>(2002), Cecilia Vicu&ntilde;a exhibited visual art at the Museo Nacional de Bellas Artes in Santiago, Chile.</p>"},{"id":"f33f5968-d2e2-4388-b885-84f221b52917","content":"<p>Cecilia Vicu&ntilde;a is a true multidisciplinary artist whose works include numerous poetry collections and visual art exhibitions.</p>"},{"id":"a3f5ca12-e88a-4c39-9379-cc3cadf47502","content":"<p>Published in 1983 by Tanam Press, <em>Precario/Precarious</em> is a collection of poetry by the multidisciplinary artist Cecilia Vicu&ntilde;a.</p>"},{"id":"d2ed9d21-39ff-48f1-b4d5-9808d8a1ccd1","content":"<p>In 1971, Cecilia Vicu&ntilde;a exhibited her first solo art exhibition, <em>Pinturas, poemas y explicaciones</em>, in Chile, her country of birth.</p>"}],"keys":["a3f5ca12-e88a-4c39-9379-cc3cadf47502"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence effectively introduces the poetry collection <em>Precario/Precarious</em>, noting that it is a collection by Vicu&ntilde;a that was published in 1983 by Tanam Press.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence mentions the 1983 poetry collection <em>Precario/Precarious</em>, it focuses mainly on Vicu&ntilde;a&rsquo;s visual art. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence doesn&rsquo;t introduce the 1983 poetry collection <em>Precario/Precarious</em>; instead, it introduces Vicu&ntilde;a. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the location of Vicu&ntilde;a&rsquo;s 1971 exhibition <em>Pinturas, poemas y explicaciones</em>; it doesn&rsquo;t introduce the 1983 poetry collection <em>Precario/Precarious</em><em>.</em></p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1691007959347,"updateDate":1691007959347}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959347, 1691007959347),
    ($SATQ$a204d618$SATQ$, $SATQ$cb8c5ab8-a024-4c2e-beb0-944a45d428c3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 1949, Frank Zamboni developed an ice rink resurfacing machine. As Zamboni&rsquo;s machine moved along the rink&rsquo;s surface, it first scraped off the top layer of ice. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> it sprayed water into the deep grooves left behind by customers&rsquo; skates. Lastly, it smoothed over the newly formed ice.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bf34ac3d-9080-4def-a0c4-22f8adc96599","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"c883f677-ce0d-4324-9f33-d45637159ab8","content":"<p>Next,</p>"},{"id":"ffeb8324-1970-4ec5-9e45-191ddb200fff","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"935616c8-210b-4af0-890e-909e3b33702b","content":"<p>In contrast,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c883f677-ce0d-4324-9f33-d45637159ab8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. "Next" logically signals that the action in this sentence&mdash;the water spraying&mdash;is the next step in the resurfacing process, following the ice scraping mentioned in the previous sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "for example" illogically signals that the action in this sentence is an example of the action in the previous sentence. Instead, the water spraying is the next step in a process that begins with the ice scraping. Choice C is incorrect because "similarly" illogically signals that the action in this sentence is similar to the action in the previous sentence. Instead, the water spraying is the next step in a process that begins with the ice scraping. Choice D is incorrect because "in contrast" illogically signals that the action in this sentence contrasts with the action in the previous sentence. Instead, the water spraying is the next step in a process that begins with the ice scraping.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a204d618","external_id":"cb8c5ab8-a024-4c2e-beb0-944a45d428c3","vaultid":null,"uId":"cab47a73-871a-48b6-a2a4-0c442fe36d7b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 1949, Frank Zamboni developed an ice rink resurfacing machine. As Zamboni&rsquo;s machine moved along the rink&rsquo;s surface, it first scraped off the top layer of ice. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> it sprayed water into the deep grooves left behind by customers&rsquo; skates. Lastly, it smoothed over the newly formed ice.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bf34ac3d-9080-4def-a0c4-22f8adc96599","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"c883f677-ce0d-4324-9f33-d45637159ab8","content":"<p>Next,</p>"},{"id":"ffeb8324-1970-4ec5-9e45-191ddb200fff","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"935616c8-210b-4af0-890e-909e3b33702b","content":"<p>In contrast,</p>"}],"keys":["c883f677-ce0d-4324-9f33-d45637159ab8"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. \"Next\" logically signals that the action in this sentence&mdash;the water spraying&mdash;is the next step in the resurfacing process, following the ice scraping mentioned in the previous sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"for example\" illogically signals that the action in this sentence is an example of the action in the previous sentence. Instead, the water spraying is the next step in a process that begins with the ice scraping. Choice C is incorrect because \"similarly\" illogically signals that the action in this sentence is similar to the action in the previous sentence. Instead, the water spraying is the next step in a process that begins with the ice scraping. Choice D is incorrect because \"in contrast\" illogically signals that the action in this sentence contrasts with the action in the previous sentence. Instead, the water spraying is the next step in a process that begins with the ice scraping.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323043,"updateDate":1730147323043}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323043, 1730147323043),
    ($SATQ$a266d876$SATQ$, $SATQ$4ed2b7c1-cbad-481a-9974-80cdb6245839$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Upper-atmospheric jets&mdash;phenomena whereby electrical discharges cause narrow cones of blue light to briefly burst upward from the tops of thunderclouds&mdash;have been observed reaching the ionosphere. The extreme altitudes involved (the ionosphere begins about 80 km above Earth) mark these gigantic jets as outliers; <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the majority of jets reach heights of only 20 to 50 km.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3e959e0f-7ea2-42cf-b55a-717d6d51d0ac","content":"<p>nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"9ac307e4-3f17-4d9c-a224-fdd644db119d","content":"<p>consequently,</p>"},{"id":"a73d866b-c8b5-4ea2-a829-59bc31d81869","content":"<p>indeed,</p>"},{"id":"2c80eba6-3f5d-40b2-8f19-ac82db2477ce","content":"<p>in addition,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a73d866b-c8b5-4ea2-a829-59bc31d81869"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;Indeed&rdquo; logically signals that the information that follows&mdash;that the majority of jets reach heights of only 20 to 50 km&mdash;offers additional emphasis in support of the previous claim that jets reaching the ionosphere (about 80 km above Earth) are outliers.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;nevertheless&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about most jets reaching heights of only 20 to 50 km contrasts with the previous claim that jets reaching the ionosphere are outliers. Instead, it provides additional emphasis in support of that claim. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;consequently&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about most jets reaching heights of only 20 to 50 km is a consequence, or result, of some jets being outliers. Instead, it offers additional emphasis in support of the claim that jets reaching the ionosphere are outliers. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;in addition&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about most jets reaching heights of only 20 to 50 km merely adds to the previous claim that jets reaching the ionosphere are considered outliers. Instead, it provides additional emphasis in support of that claim.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a266d876","external_id":"4ed2b7c1-cbad-481a-9974-80cdb6245839","vaultid":null,"uId":"5b3fead6-e619-45c2-8fbc-c9bbd6e63803","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Upper-atmospheric jets&mdash;phenomena whereby electrical discharges cause narrow cones of blue light to briefly burst upward from the tops of thunderclouds&mdash;have been observed reaching the ionosphere. The extreme altitudes involved (the ionosphere begins about 80 km above Earth) mark these gigantic jets as outliers; <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the majority of jets reach heights of only 20 to 50 km.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3e959e0f-7ea2-42cf-b55a-717d6d51d0ac","content":"<p>nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"9ac307e4-3f17-4d9c-a224-fdd644db119d","content":"<p>consequently,</p>"},{"id":"a73d866b-c8b5-4ea2-a829-59bc31d81869","content":"<p>indeed,</p>"},{"id":"2c80eba6-3f5d-40b2-8f19-ac82db2477ce","content":"<p>in addition,</p>"}],"keys":["a73d866b-c8b5-4ea2-a829-59bc31d81869"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;Indeed&rdquo; logically signals that the information that follows&mdash;that the majority of jets reach heights of only 20 to 50 km&mdash;offers additional emphasis in support of the previous claim that jets reaching the ionosphere (about 80 km above Earth) are outliers.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;nevertheless&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about most jets reaching heights of only 20 to 50 km contrasts with the previous claim that jets reaching the ionosphere are outliers. Instead, it provides additional emphasis in support of that claim. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;consequently&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about most jets reaching heights of only 20 to 50 km is a consequence, or result, of some jets being outliers. Instead, it offers additional emphasis in support of the claim that jets reaching the ionosphere are outliers. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;in addition&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about most jets reaching heights of only 20 to 50 km merely adds to the previous claim that jets reaching the ionosphere are considered outliers. Instead, it provides additional emphasis in support of that claim.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771168,"updateDate":1776362771168}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771168, 1776362771168),
    ($SATQ$a2bff07e$SATQ$, $SATQ$fa45fdd1-8a89-477b-be0c-783d930d6e6b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Economist Elinor Ostrom&rsquo;s studies of communities around the world have empirically demonstrated that common pool resources, such as grazing lands, can be sustainably managed by the people who use them (rather than through private entities or centralized governments). <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Ostrom&rsquo;s work is a repudiation of the &ldquo;tragedy of the commons,&rdquo; the view that individuals will inevitably overexploit a finite shared resource if given unfettered access to it. &nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"932a9e38-3b92-4973-ade3-49df42a1e9a1","content":"<p>By contrast,</p>"},{"id":"d14a5403-2089-4c96-904e-15a60729eb77","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"1b705354-a161-4e38-89b7-62c0d03b6d00","content":"<p>That said,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"7d0cd248-abd1-46f1-9c17-6cae2981b7f0","content":"<p>As such,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7d0cd248-abd1-46f1-9c17-6cae2981b7f0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "As such" correctly signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that Ostrom&rsquo;s work is a repudiation of the "tragedy of the commons" view&mdash;follows logically from the information about Ostrom&rsquo;s studies in the previous sentence. According to that sentence, Ostrom&rsquo;s studies demonstrate that common pool resources can in fact be sustainably managed by the people who use them.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "by contrast" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the information about Ostrom&rsquo;s studies in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim that Ostrom&rsquo;s work repudiates the "tragedy of the commons" view follows logically from that information. Choice B is incorrect because "for example" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence exemplifies the information about Ostrom&rsquo;s studies in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim that Ostrom&rsquo;s work repudiates the "tragedy of the commons" view follows logically from that information. Choice C is incorrect because "that said" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is an exception or caveat to the information about Ostrom&rsquo;s studies in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim that Ostrom&rsquo;s work repudiates the "tragedy of the commons" view follows logically from that information.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a2bff07e","external_id":"fa45fdd1-8a89-477b-be0c-783d930d6e6b","vaultid":null,"uId":"8aff656a-6bdb-4cc6-b526-ac49d0ef6fef","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Economist Elinor Ostrom&rsquo;s studies of communities around the world have empirically demonstrated that common pool resources, such as grazing lands, can be sustainably managed by the people who use them (rather than through private entities or centralized governments). <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Ostrom&rsquo;s work is a repudiation of the &ldquo;tragedy of the commons,&rdquo; the view that individuals will inevitably overexploit a finite shared resource if given unfettered access to it. &nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"932a9e38-3b92-4973-ade3-49df42a1e9a1","content":"<p>By contrast,</p>"},{"id":"d14a5403-2089-4c96-904e-15a60729eb77","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"1b705354-a161-4e38-89b7-62c0d03b6d00","content":"<p>That said,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"7d0cd248-abd1-46f1-9c17-6cae2981b7f0","content":"<p>As such,</p>"}],"keys":["7d0cd248-abd1-46f1-9c17-6cae2981b7f0"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"As such\" correctly signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that Ostrom&rsquo;s work is a repudiation of the \"tragedy of the commons\" view&mdash;follows logically from the information about Ostrom&rsquo;s studies in the previous sentence. According to that sentence, Ostrom&rsquo;s studies demonstrate that common pool resources can in fact be sustainably managed by the people who use them.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"by contrast\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the information about Ostrom&rsquo;s studies in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim that Ostrom&rsquo;s work repudiates the \"tragedy of the commons\" view follows logically from that information. Choice B is incorrect because \"for example\" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence exemplifies the information about Ostrom&rsquo;s studies in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim that Ostrom&rsquo;s work repudiates the \"tragedy of the commons\" view follows logically from that information. Choice C is incorrect because \"that said\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is an exception or caveat to the information about Ostrom&rsquo;s studies in the previous sentence. Instead, the claim that Ostrom&rsquo;s work repudiates the \"tragedy of the commons\" view follows logically from that information.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669215,"updateDate":1743430669215}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669215, 1743430669215),
    ($SATQ$a3204ab0$SATQ$, $SATQ$8be51932-52d5-439e-989c-054212dee742$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e4c8e83d-f68b-451a-a8ee-1d7200a8eb3c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Yellowstone is a national park in the northwest United States.</li>
<li>In 1995, gray wolves were reintroduced into the park.</li>
<li>Since then, the number of gray wolves in the park has stabilized at roughly 100.</li>
<li>This number is believed to be the park&rsquo;s carrying capacity.</li>
<li>Carrying capacity describes the maximum number of a species that a specific environment&rsquo;s resources can sustain over time.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to specify the number of gray wolves in Yellowstone. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"32dca355-4bf0-4f23-9873-7056566c840c","content":"<p>Gray wolves were reintroduced into Yellowstone, a national park in the northwest United States, in 1995.</p>"},{"id":"de4dfc77-7760-49e5-acf7-db990ef3ddaa","content":"<p>As of 1995, there were gray wolves living in Yellowstone, a national park in the northwest United States.</p>"},{"id":"2cbf4eec-a9bc-4cd3-82a6-238a62dea5d6","content":"<p>The carrying capacity of an environment, such as Yellowstone, describes the maximum number of species that the environment can sustain over time.</p>"},{"id":"d75af13e-93b8-4650-abf8-8d3c3a65e045","content":"<p>Yellowstone is a national park that has roughly 100 gray wolves living in it.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d75af13e-93b8-4650-abf8-8d3c3a65e045"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. This choice uses relevant information from the third bullet point to state the approximate number of gray wolves in Yellowstone. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice mentions the year gray wolves in Yellowstone were reintroduced but not how many there are currently. Choice B is incorrect. This choice mentions the year gray wolves in Yellowstone were reintroduced but not how many there are currently. Choice C is incorrect. This choice defines the term carrying capacity but doesn&rsquo;t connect it to the specific number of gray wolves currently living in Yellowstone. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-SYN-E009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$cb432f97-125b-4a3a-afdb-470e750c5824$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a3204ab0","external_id":"8be51932-52d5-439e-989c-054212dee742","vaultid":"e4c8e83d-f68b-451a-a8ee-1d7200a8eb3c","uId":"2ff3051b-a595-4f77-b355-6fde49671082","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Yellowstone is a national park in the northwest United States.</li>\n<li>In 1995, gray wolves were reintroduced into the park.</li>\n<li>Since then, the number of gray wolves in the park has stabilized at roughly 100.</li>\n<li>This number is believed to be the park&rsquo;s carrying capacity.</li>\n<li>Carrying capacity describes the maximum number of a species that a specific environment&rsquo;s resources can sustain over time.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to specify the number of gray wolves in Yellowstone. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"32dca355-4bf0-4f23-9873-7056566c840c","content":"<p>Gray wolves were reintroduced into Yellowstone, a national park in the northwest United States, in 1995.</p>"},{"id":"de4dfc77-7760-49e5-acf7-db990ef3ddaa","content":"<p>As of 1995, there were gray wolves living in Yellowstone, a national park in the northwest United States.</p>"},{"id":"2cbf4eec-a9bc-4cd3-82a6-238a62dea5d6","content":"<p>The carrying capacity of an environment, such as Yellowstone, describes the maximum number of species that the environment can sustain over time.</p>"},{"id":"d75af13e-93b8-4650-abf8-8d3c3a65e045","content":"<p>Yellowstone is a national park that has roughly 100 gray wolves living in it.</p>"}],"keys":["d75af13e-93b8-4650-abf8-8d3c3a65e045"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. This choice uses relevant information from the third bullet point to state the approximate number of gray wolves in Yellowstone. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice mentions the year gray wolves in Yellowstone were reintroduced but not how many there are currently. Choice B is incorrect. This choice mentions the year gray wolves in Yellowstone were reintroduced but not how many there are currently. Choice C is incorrect. This choice defines the term carrying capacity but doesn&rsquo;t connect it to the specific number of gray wolves currently living in Yellowstone. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-SYN-E009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"cb432f97-125b-4a3a-afdb-470e750c5824","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959371,"updateDate":1691007959371}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959371, 1691007959371),
    ($SATQ$a3bf0a9d$SATQ$, $SATQ$ce846d6a-1094-4244-a4b0-8eeb2638fd10$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 2021, a model developed by astrophysicist Catherine Zucker and her research team revealed that the same supernovas responsible for the creation and ongoing expansion of the Local Bubble&mdash;a 14-million-year-old cavity in the Milky Way&mdash;are likely responsible for the formation of new stars. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> this model detailed how the bubble&rsquo;s expansion trapped interstellar clouds of gas and dust that became stars upon their eventual collapse.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c7b5c78e-c11d-4aaf-982f-a737092b6ddf","content":"<p>Hence,</p>"},{"id":"ab6ca2d3-2591-4654-9c2d-b5497c4c8269","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"c849bfa8-ee14-477b-9e23-ed8bbde28199","content":"<p>Admittedly,</p>"},{"id":"7443f9b4-0001-4026-bce2-ee35bd6f8012","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7443f9b4-0001-4026-bce2-ee35bd6f8012"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "Specifically" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that the Local Bubble&rsquo;s expansion trapped clouds of gas and dust that formed new stars&mdash;provides specific, precise details elaborating on the more general information in the previous sentence about the relationship between the Local Bubble&rsquo;s expansion and the formation of new stars.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "hence" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a result of the information in the previous sentence about the relationship between the Local Bubble&rsquo;s expansion and the formation of new stars. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on that information. Choice B is incorrect because "however" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the information in the previous sentence about the relationship between the Local Bubble&rsquo;s expansion and the formation of new stars. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on that information. Choice C is incorrect because "admittedly" illogically signals that the information in this sentence provides an exception or caveat to the previous information about the relationship between the Local Bubble&rsquo;s expansion and the formation of new stars. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on that information.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a3bf0a9d","external_id":"ce846d6a-1094-4244-a4b0-8eeb2638fd10","vaultid":null,"uId":"e98be023-94e2-4542-83b4-fc3211d8dce1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 2021, a model developed by astrophysicist Catherine Zucker and her research team revealed that the same supernovas responsible for the creation and ongoing expansion of the Local Bubble&mdash;a 14-million-year-old cavity in the Milky Way&mdash;are likely responsible for the formation of new stars. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> this model detailed how the bubble&rsquo;s expansion trapped interstellar clouds of gas and dust that became stars upon their eventual collapse.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c7b5c78e-c11d-4aaf-982f-a737092b6ddf","content":"<p>Hence,</p>"},{"id":"ab6ca2d3-2591-4654-9c2d-b5497c4c8269","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"c849bfa8-ee14-477b-9e23-ed8bbde28199","content":"<p>Admittedly,</p>"},{"id":"7443f9b4-0001-4026-bce2-ee35bd6f8012","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"}],"keys":["7443f9b4-0001-4026-bce2-ee35bd6f8012"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"Specifically\" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that the Local Bubble&rsquo;s expansion trapped clouds of gas and dust that formed new stars&mdash;provides specific, precise details elaborating on the more general information in the previous sentence about the relationship between the Local Bubble&rsquo;s expansion and the formation of new stars.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"hence\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a result of the information in the previous sentence about the relationship between the Local Bubble&rsquo;s expansion and the formation of new stars. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on that information. Choice B is incorrect because \"however\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the information in the previous sentence about the relationship between the Local Bubble&rsquo;s expansion and the formation of new stars. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on that information. Choice C is incorrect because \"admittedly\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence provides an exception or caveat to the previous information about the relationship between the Local Bubble&rsquo;s expansion and the formation of new stars. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on that information.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669213,"updateDate":1743430669213}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669213, 1743430669213),
    ($SATQ$a40c7aa3$SATQ$, $SATQ$4338630c-fbdc-4eb4-becf-2800b705feb6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$0be635bf-04b3-4efb-a953-ba66f85e1e41$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Most of the planets that have been discovered outside our solar system orbit G-type stars, like our Sun. In 2014, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> researchers identified a planet orbiting KELT-9, a B-type star more than twice as massive and nearly twice as hot as the Sun. Called KELT-9b, it is one of the hottest planets ever discovered.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"51887696-623d-4825-94d9-77392942363e","content":"<p>likewise,</p>"},{"id":"1b0b8aa9-6562-4d28-bcab-af760589e58b","content":"<p>however,</p>"},{"id":"474ca528-d8eb-4958-8e3d-a0bfbe77b0b0","content":"<p>therefore,</p>"},{"id":"4110e3a9-dca0-4424-9e2d-8a68424fc896","content":"<p>for example,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1b0b8aa9-6562-4d28-bcab-af760589e58b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The word &ldquo;however&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence about the planet KELT-9b&mdash;that it orbits a B-type star&mdash;contrasts with the previous information about planets discovered outside our solar system. Most of these planets orbit G-type stars, not B-type stars. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;likewise&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about the planet KELT-9b is similar to the previous information about most planets outside our solar system. Instead, it contrasts with that information. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;therefore&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about the planet KELT-9b is a result of the previous information about most planets outside our solar system. Instead, it contrasts with that information.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about the planet KELT-9b is an example of the previous information about most planets outside our solar system. Instead, it contrasts with that information. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-256-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$993ce3f0-c542-44f0-9c98-d8abba8b1747$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a40c7aa3","external_id":"4338630c-fbdc-4eb4-becf-2800b705feb6","vaultid":"0be635bf-04b3-4efb-a953-ba66f85e1e41","uId":"0b66987f-8fe0-4ffe-83b7-5b3ebe39b375","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Most of the planets that have been discovered outside our solar system orbit G-type stars, like our Sun. In 2014, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> researchers identified a planet orbiting KELT-9, a B-type star more than twice as massive and nearly twice as hot as the Sun. Called KELT-9b, it is one of the hottest planets ever discovered.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"51887696-623d-4825-94d9-77392942363e","content":"<p>likewise,</p>"},{"id":"1b0b8aa9-6562-4d28-bcab-af760589e58b","content":"<p>however,</p>"},{"id":"474ca528-d8eb-4958-8e3d-a0bfbe77b0b0","content":"<p>therefore,</p>"},{"id":"4110e3a9-dca0-4424-9e2d-8a68424fc896","content":"<p>for example,</p>"}],"keys":["1b0b8aa9-6562-4d28-bcab-af760589e58b"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The word &ldquo;however&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence about the planet KELT-9b&mdash;that it orbits a B-type star&mdash;contrasts with the previous information about planets discovered outside our solar system. Most of these planets orbit G-type stars, not B-type stars. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;likewise&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about the planet KELT-9b is similar to the previous information about most planets outside our solar system. Instead, it contrasts with that information. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;therefore&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about the planet KELT-9b is a result of the previous information about most planets outside our solar system. Instead, it contrasts with that information.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about the planet KELT-9b is an example of the previous information about most planets outside our solar system. Instead, it contrasts with that information. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-256-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"993ce3f0-c542-44f0-9c98-d8abba8b1747","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959333,"updateDate":1691007959333}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959333, 1691007959333),
    ($SATQ$a4366255$SATQ$, $SATQ$3cdead08-369b-4e06-9b4a-c6cd52f44a89$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Musicians around the world have used protest songs to raise awareness about human rights violations.</li>
<li>US folk singer Aunt Molly Jackson released the protest song &ldquo;Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell&rdquo; in 1932.</li>
<li>It exposed the unlivable wages and dangerous working conditions coal miners faced in Kentucky during the 1920s and 1930s.</li>
<li>South African singer-songwriter Hugh Masekela released the protest song &ldquo;Bring Him Back Home&rdquo; in 1987.</li>
<li>It called on the South African government to free Nelson Mandela, an anti-apartheid leader who&rsquo;d been unjustly imprisoned.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to contrast the song &ldquo;Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell&rdquo; with the song &ldquo;Bring Him Back Home.&rdquo; Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6daaabb6-b814-4ff6-83e4-2855a1f0a2bc","content":"<p>The songs &ldquo;Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell&rdquo; and &ldquo;Bring Him Back Home&rdquo; both raised awareness about human rights violations.</p>"},{"id":"67a134ce-b3a9-4aa1-8d45-9ad9d6976194","content":"<p>While both are protest songs, &ldquo;Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell&rdquo; is about coal miners in Kentucky, whereas &ldquo;Bring Him Back Home&rdquo; is about the anti-apartheid leader Nelson Mandela.</p>"},{"id":"d122564e-8165-4878-ad78-1b1691387ba1","content":"<p>Hugh Masekela&rsquo;s song &ldquo;Bring Him Back Home,&rdquo; released in 1987, called on the South African government to free Nelson Mandela.</p>"},{"id":"ca3fc16e-5a13-4a76-bb7d-526a23a022a4","content":"<p>Released in 1932 by Aunt Molly Jackson, the song &ldquo;Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell&rdquo; was a protest against the unlivable wages and dangerous working conditions faced by Kentucky coal miners.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["67a134ce-b3a9-4aa1-8d45-9ad9d6976194"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence contrasts the two songs, noting that "Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell" is about coal miners in Kentucky, whereas "Bring Him Back Home" is about Nelson Mandela.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between "Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell" and "Bring Him Back Home"; it doesn&rsquo;t contrast the two songs. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence provides a description of the song "Bring Him Back Home," it doesn&rsquo;t mention "Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell" or contrast the two songs. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides a description of the song "Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell," it doesn&rsquo;t mention "Bring Him Back Home" or contrast the two songs.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a4366255","external_id":"3cdead08-369b-4e06-9b4a-c6cd52f44a89","vaultid":null,"uId":"1fe302d4-5227-44a4-91e8-a93393bf226d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Musicians around the world have used protest songs to raise awareness about human rights violations.</li>\n<li>US folk singer Aunt Molly Jackson released the protest song &ldquo;Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell&rdquo; in 1932.</li>\n<li>It exposed the unlivable wages and dangerous working conditions coal miners faced in Kentucky during the 1920s and 1930s.</li>\n<li>South African singer-songwriter Hugh Masekela released the protest song &ldquo;Bring Him Back Home&rdquo; in 1987.</li>\n<li>It called on the South African government to free Nelson Mandela, an anti-apartheid leader who&rsquo;d been unjustly imprisoned.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to contrast the song &ldquo;Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell&rdquo; with the song &ldquo;Bring Him Back Home.&rdquo; Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6daaabb6-b814-4ff6-83e4-2855a1f0a2bc","content":"<p>The songs &ldquo;Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell&rdquo; and &ldquo;Bring Him Back Home&rdquo; both raised awareness about human rights violations.</p>"},{"id":"67a134ce-b3a9-4aa1-8d45-9ad9d6976194","content":"<p>While both are protest songs, &ldquo;Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell&rdquo; is about coal miners in Kentucky, whereas &ldquo;Bring Him Back Home&rdquo; is about the anti-apartheid leader Nelson Mandela.</p>"},{"id":"d122564e-8165-4878-ad78-1b1691387ba1","content":"<p>Hugh Masekela&rsquo;s song &ldquo;Bring Him Back Home,&rdquo; released in 1987, called on the South African government to free Nelson Mandela.</p>"},{"id":"ca3fc16e-5a13-4a76-bb7d-526a23a022a4","content":"<p>Released in 1932 by Aunt Molly Jackson, the song &ldquo;Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell&rdquo; was a protest against the unlivable wages and dangerous working conditions faced by Kentucky coal miners.</p>"}],"keys":["67a134ce-b3a9-4aa1-8d45-9ad9d6976194"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence contrasts the two songs, noting that \"Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell\" is about coal miners in Kentucky, whereas \"Bring Him Back Home\" is about Nelson Mandela.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between \"Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell\" and \"Bring Him Back Home\"; it doesn&rsquo;t contrast the two songs. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence provides a description of the song \"Bring Him Back Home,\" it doesn&rsquo;t mention \"Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell\" or contrast the two songs. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides a description of the song \"Poor Miner&rsquo;s Farewell,\" it doesn&rsquo;t mention \"Bring Him Back Home\" or contrast the two songs.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669205,"updateDate":1743430669205}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669205, 1743430669205),
    ($SATQ$a6155e60$SATQ$, $SATQ$527a6e1b-a044-4d40-9daf-7f1502b3cb97$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f07a00fb-fa07-45f6-a9dd-0f20d92a0698$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Every chemical compound has a spectroscopic fingerprint, a pattern of reflected light unique to that compound. <span aria-hidden="true" class="item-blank">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> upon analyzing the light reflected by the bright regions on the surface of the dwarf planet Ceres, Maria Cristina De Sanctis of Rome&rsquo;s National Institute of Astrophysics was able to determine that the regions contain large amounts of the compound sodium carbonate.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e47e1d70-b425-431c-bad5-10ee84780a7f","content":"<p>Regardless,</p>"},{"id":"531332de-410e-4039-bbb6-093ce2f90f29","content":"<p>Meanwhile,</p>"},{"id":"c6aa47f5-0416-4655-b1b3-01d7663e0c4c","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"},{"id":"05969ee8-795f-439b-b336-aca20f85e55c","content":"<p>In comparison,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c6aa47f5-0416-4655-b1b3-01d7663e0c4c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;Thus&rdquo; logically signals that the action described in this sentence&mdash;the researcher being able to determine the chemical makeup of the planet&rsquo;s bright regions based on how they reflect light&mdash;is a result or consequence of the previous information about spectroscopic fingerprints. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;regardless&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence occurs despite the previous information about spectroscopic fingerprints. Instead, the finding in this sentence is a result or consequence of that information. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;meanwhile&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence either occurs at the same time as or offers an alternative to the previous information about spectroscopic fingerprints. Instead, the finding in this sentence is a result or consequence of that information. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;in comparison&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence is being compared with the previous information about spectroscopic fingerprints. Instead, the finding in this sentence is a result or consequence of that information. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-202-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c433a28a-3038-4728-8d7a-1e7aaa7a41ad$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a6155e60","external_id":"527a6e1b-a044-4d40-9daf-7f1502b3cb97","vaultid":"f07a00fb-fa07-45f6-a9dd-0f20d92a0698","uId":"67e92e46-3b8a-4b22-bdd8-443c482bc376","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Every chemical compound has a spectroscopic fingerprint, a pattern of reflected light unique to that compound. <span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"item-blank\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> upon analyzing the light reflected by the bright regions on the surface of the dwarf planet Ceres, Maria Cristina De Sanctis of Rome&rsquo;s National Institute of Astrophysics was able to determine that the regions contain large amounts of the compound sodium carbonate.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e47e1d70-b425-431c-bad5-10ee84780a7f","content":"<p>Regardless,</p>"},{"id":"531332de-410e-4039-bbb6-093ce2f90f29","content":"<p>Meanwhile,</p>"},{"id":"c6aa47f5-0416-4655-b1b3-01d7663e0c4c","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"},{"id":"05969ee8-795f-439b-b336-aca20f85e55c","content":"<p>In comparison,</p>"}],"keys":["c6aa47f5-0416-4655-b1b3-01d7663e0c4c"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;Thus&rdquo; logically signals that the action described in this sentence&mdash;the researcher being able to determine the chemical makeup of the planet&rsquo;s bright regions based on how they reflect light&mdash;is a result or consequence of the previous information about spectroscopic fingerprints. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;regardless&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence occurs despite the previous information about spectroscopic fingerprints. Instead, the finding in this sentence is a result or consequence of that information. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;meanwhile&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence either occurs at the same time as or offers an alternative to the previous information about spectroscopic fingerprints. Instead, the finding in this sentence is a result or consequence of that information. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;in comparison&rdquo; illogically signals that the action described in this sentence is being compared with the previous information about spectroscopic fingerprints. Instead, the finding in this sentence is a result or consequence of that information. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-202-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c433a28a-3038-4728-8d7a-1e7aaa7a41ad","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959325,"updateDate":1691007959325}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959325, 1691007959325),
    ($SATQ$a773f069$SATQ$, $SATQ$c4246267-7754-4e8d-aff4-b2cb9a88db5f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$93b26a6e-5877-4d3e-9328-8a57e4cf7adc$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Small, flat structures called spatulae are found at the tips of the hairs on a spider&rsquo;s leg. These spatulae temporarily bond with the atoms of whatever they touch. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> spiders are able to cling to and climb almost any surface.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0dd7c71c-c17d-4645-b3b1-36040f032a76","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"},{"id":"06f95451-057d-4ff5-b5ce-714a315269c3","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"9137cc7c-e312-42b6-81d7-542fe94c4ab8","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"40d6fba4-638a-4bbe-be2b-55b671f2f26e","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["40d6fba4-638a-4bbe-be2b-55b671f2f26e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;As a result&rdquo; logically signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that spiders can cling to and climb almost any surface&mdash;is because of the previous information about the bonding properties of spiders&rsquo; spatulae. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;for instance&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence exemplifies the information in the previous sentences. Instead, the claim is because of the previous information about the bonding properties of spiders&rsquo; spatulae. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence contrasts with the information in the previous sentences. Instead, the claim is because of the previous information about the bonding properties of spiders&rsquo; spatulae. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;similarly&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is similar to, but separate from, the information in the previous sentences. Instead, the claim is because of the previous information about the bonding properties of spiders&rsquo; spatulae. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-225-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$38fbd87d-3c86-47a3-9bdf-6505ae9b2696$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a773f069","external_id":"c4246267-7754-4e8d-aff4-b2cb9a88db5f","vaultid":"93b26a6e-5877-4d3e-9328-8a57e4cf7adc","uId":"2ece1619-5b8a-4571-8efd-42014192e122","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Small, flat structures called spatulae are found at the tips of the hairs on a spider&rsquo;s leg. These spatulae temporarily bond with the atoms of whatever they touch. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> spiders are able to cling to and climb almost any surface.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0dd7c71c-c17d-4645-b3b1-36040f032a76","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"},{"id":"06f95451-057d-4ff5-b5ce-714a315269c3","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"9137cc7c-e312-42b6-81d7-542fe94c4ab8","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"40d6fba4-638a-4bbe-be2b-55b671f2f26e","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"}],"keys":["40d6fba4-638a-4bbe-be2b-55b671f2f26e"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;As a result&rdquo; logically signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that spiders can cling to and climb almost any surface&mdash;is because of the previous information about the bonding properties of spiders&rsquo; spatulae. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;for instance&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence exemplifies the information in the previous sentences. Instead, the claim is because of the previous information about the bonding properties of spiders&rsquo; spatulae. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence contrasts with the information in the previous sentences. Instead, the claim is because of the previous information about the bonding properties of spiders&rsquo; spatulae. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;similarly&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is similar to, but separate from, the information in the previous sentences. Instead, the claim is because of the previous information about the bonding properties of spiders&rsquo; spatulae. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-225-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"38fbd87d-3c86-47a3-9bdf-6505ae9b2696","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959332,"updateDate":1691007959332}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959332, 1691007959332),
    ($SATQ$a7a944ed$SATQ$, $SATQ$7c141757-c5fa-480a-8a3c-87b8746c8d3b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In astrophysics, a ring of debris orbiting a larger object within the object&rsquo;s Roche limit is expected to persist as a ring, whereas a ring of debris orbiting outside this limit would likely accrete into a satellite (<span data-ssml-sub-alias="e g">e.g.</span>, a moon). Bruno Morgado and colleagues, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> detected a dense ring of material orbiting the trans-Neptunian object Quaoar at a distance of 2,500 miles, well outside the calculated Roche limit of 1,100 miles, that has remained intact.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"61534c72-7b8e-4aeb-a0fc-6b4366029f51","content":"<p>though,</p>"},{"id":"30e1328d-dd84-42aa-a2b8-05996525137f","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"e3d7b402-b262-40a3-a51e-07b879c55e49","content":"<p>fittingly,</p>"},{"id":"f534a2c6-4823-4f5a-be8a-cf5686b243e8","content":"<p>likewise,&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["61534c72-7b8e-4aeb-a0fc-6b4366029f51"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Though&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that Morgado and colleagues detected a dense ring orbiting outside the Roche limit that remained intact&mdash;presents an exception to the previous sentence&rsquo;s claim about debris orbiting outside an object&rsquo;s Roche limit. Such debris would be expected to accrete into a satellite, not remain intact as a ring.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Morgado&rsquo;s discovery exemplifies the previous sentence&rsquo;s claim about debris orbiting outside an object&rsquo;s Roche limit. Instead, the sentence presents an exception to the claim. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;fittingly&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Morgado&rsquo;s discovery aligns with the previous sentence&rsquo;s claim about debris orbiting outside an object&rsquo;s Roche limit. Instead, the sentence presents an exception to the claim. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;likewise&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Morgado&rsquo;s discovery is similar to the previous sentence&rsquo;s claim about debris orbiting outside an object&rsquo;s Roche limit. Instead, the sentence presents an exception to the claim.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a7a944ed","external_id":"7c141757-c5fa-480a-8a3c-87b8746c8d3b","vaultid":null,"uId":"79c7e0a2-f33b-46e3-9ca0-37c055687d97","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In astrophysics, a ring of debris orbiting a larger object within the object&rsquo;s Roche limit is expected to persist as a ring, whereas a ring of debris orbiting outside this limit would likely accrete into a satellite (<span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"e g\">e.g.</span>, a moon). Bruno Morgado and colleagues, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> detected a dense ring of material orbiting the trans-Neptunian object Quaoar at a distance of 2,500 miles, well outside the calculated Roche limit of 1,100 miles, that has remained intact.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"61534c72-7b8e-4aeb-a0fc-6b4366029f51","content":"<p>though,</p>"},{"id":"30e1328d-dd84-42aa-a2b8-05996525137f","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"e3d7b402-b262-40a3-a51e-07b879c55e49","content":"<p>fittingly,</p>"},{"id":"f534a2c6-4823-4f5a-be8a-cf5686b243e8","content":"<p>likewise,&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["61534c72-7b8e-4aeb-a0fc-6b4366029f51"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Though&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that Morgado and colleagues detected a dense ring orbiting outside the Roche limit that remained intact&mdash;presents an exception to the previous sentence&rsquo;s claim about debris orbiting outside an object&rsquo;s Roche limit. Such debris would be expected to accrete into a satellite, not remain intact as a ring.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Morgado&rsquo;s discovery exemplifies the previous sentence&rsquo;s claim about debris orbiting outside an object&rsquo;s Roche limit. Instead, the sentence presents an exception to the claim. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;fittingly&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Morgado&rsquo;s discovery aligns with the previous sentence&rsquo;s claim about debris orbiting outside an object&rsquo;s Roche limit. Instead, the sentence presents an exception to the claim. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;likewise&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Morgado&rsquo;s discovery is similar to the previous sentence&rsquo;s claim about debris orbiting outside an object&rsquo;s Roche limit. Instead, the sentence presents an exception to the claim.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$a819d8b6$SATQ$, $SATQ$557b744d-7231-424e-af00-30e5d4008971$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a2027d67-de2c-407c-a4f2-3909e845094a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 1873, Spanish scientist Santiago Ram&oacute;n y Cajal observed that brain fibers have distinct boundaries with clear end points, a finding that went against earlier assumptions about the brain. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> scientists had assumed that the brain was a continuous web of fused fibers, not a vast network of distinct, individual cells.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"19a5ac47-54fa-46d1-9465-565d4198caa7","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"80c5f040-baf1-4608-a978-5b189099fed6","content":"<p>Previously,</p>"},{"id":"4e60d006-3f27-49a3-84fc-f400a6a197c4","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"59b2d935-b48e-417e-8ce5-438811ddb811","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["80c5f040-baf1-4608-a978-5b189099fed6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Previously&rdquo; logically signals that the fused fiber theory came before Ram&oacute;n y Cajal&rsquo;s discovery. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. &ldquo;However&rdquo; illogically signals that the fused fiber theory in this sentence contrasts with the information in the previous sentence. While this theory does contrast with Ram&oacute;n y Cajal&rsquo;s discovery, the previous sentence concludes by stating that his discovery went against prior assumptions about the brain. The fact that the fused fiber theory was one of those earlier assumptions makes &ldquo;however&rdquo; an illogical choice. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;as a result&rdquo; illogically signals that the fused fiber theory in this sentence was a result of the discovery in the previous sentence. Instead, the fused fiber theory came before Ram&oacute;n y Cajal&rsquo;s discovery. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;likewise&rdquo; illogically signals that the fused fiber theory in this sentence was similar to the discovery in the previous sentence. Instead, the fused fiber theory, which came before Ram&oacute;n y Cajal&rsquo;s discovery, was very different from it. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-246-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$41fd48d2-6864-4a83-9ea0-7c8e4410cb3a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a819d8b6","external_id":"557b744d-7231-424e-af00-30e5d4008971","vaultid":"a2027d67-de2c-407c-a4f2-3909e845094a","uId":"11daa217-03f8-41f4-93db-cb2b1f925a68","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 1873, Spanish scientist Santiago Ram&oacute;n y Cajal observed that brain fibers have distinct boundaries with clear end points, a finding that went against earlier assumptions about the brain. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> scientists had assumed that the brain was a continuous web of fused fibers, not a vast network of distinct, individual cells.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"19a5ac47-54fa-46d1-9465-565d4198caa7","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"80c5f040-baf1-4608-a978-5b189099fed6","content":"<p>Previously,</p>"},{"id":"4e60d006-3f27-49a3-84fc-f400a6a197c4","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"59b2d935-b48e-417e-8ce5-438811ddb811","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"}],"keys":["80c5f040-baf1-4608-a978-5b189099fed6"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Previously&rdquo; logically signals that the fused fiber theory came before Ram&oacute;n y Cajal&rsquo;s discovery. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. &ldquo;However&rdquo; illogically signals that the fused fiber theory in this sentence contrasts with the information in the previous sentence. While this theory does contrast with Ram&oacute;n y Cajal&rsquo;s discovery, the previous sentence concludes by stating that his discovery went against prior assumptions about the brain. The fact that the fused fiber theory was one of those earlier assumptions makes &ldquo;however&rdquo; an illogical choice. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;as a result&rdquo; illogically signals that the fused fiber theory in this sentence was a result of the discovery in the previous sentence. Instead, the fused fiber theory came before Ram&oacute;n y Cajal&rsquo;s discovery. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;likewise&rdquo; illogically signals that the fused fiber theory in this sentence was similar to the discovery in the previous sentence. Instead, the fused fiber theory, which came before Ram&oacute;n y Cajal&rsquo;s discovery, was very different from it. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-246-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"41fd48d2-6864-4a83-9ea0-7c8e4410cb3a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959333,"updateDate":1691007959333}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959333, 1691007959333),
    ($SATQ$a86c0b1b$SATQ$, $SATQ$b89a8975-4a42-461e-99fd-0fd1b7a2ad8d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$cd015e94-c4aa-4a8f-b859-5c6f668e652d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Ancient Native American and Australian Aboriginal cultures described the Pleiades star cluster as having seven stars.</li>
<li>It was referred to as the Seven Sisters in the mythology of ancient Greece.</li>
<li>Today, the cluster appears to have only six stars.</li>
<li>Two of the stars have moved so close together that they now appear as one.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to specify the reason the Pleiades&rsquo; appearance changed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b5576c18-8dcf-4a79-9394-dc9b70b61d26","content":"<p>Ancient Native American and Australian Aboriginal cultures described the Pleiades, which was referred to in Greek mythology as the Seven Sisters, as having seven stars.</p>"},{"id":"32894d46-6a1a-413f-b1e1-2f62f508c431","content":"<p>Although once referred to as the Seven Sisters, the Pleiades appears to have only six stars today.</p>"},{"id":"6b373eb3-8542-4816-a6d7-1b48c14d4bc8","content":"<p>In the time since ancient cultures described the Pleiades as having seven stars, two of the cluster&rsquo;s stars have moved so close together that they now appear as one.</p>"},{"id":"ef016a7b-79fa-4947-b0e9-7323cda88b25","content":"<p>The Pleiades has seven stars, but two are so close together that they appear to be a single star.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6b373eb3-8542-4816-a6d7-1b48c14d4bc8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence specifies the reason the Pleiades&rsquo; appearance changed, noting that two of the cluster&rsquo;s stars have moved so close together that they now appear as one star. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence specifies how ancient Native American and Australian Aboriginal cultures described the Pleiades; it doesn&rsquo;t specify the reason the Pleiades&rsquo; appearance changed.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes the appearance of the Pleiades today; it doesn&rsquo;t specify the reason the Pleiades&rsquo; appearance changed. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence explains why two of the Pleiades&rsquo; stars appear to be a single star; it doesn&rsquo;t specify the reason the Pleiades&rsquo; appearance changed. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-784-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c1f2d91d-3af7-4929-aa28-c543c7056143$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a86c0b1b","external_id":"b89a8975-4a42-461e-99fd-0fd1b7a2ad8d","vaultid":"cd015e94-c4aa-4a8f-b859-5c6f668e652d","uId":"d3315001-1b6b-47b3-a8f8-e490f04b607c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ancient Native American and Australian Aboriginal cultures described the Pleiades star cluster as having seven stars.</li>\n<li>It was referred to as the Seven Sisters in the mythology of ancient Greece.</li>\n<li>Today, the cluster appears to have only six stars.</li>\n<li>Two of the stars have moved so close together that they now appear as one.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to specify the reason the Pleiades&rsquo; appearance changed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b5576c18-8dcf-4a79-9394-dc9b70b61d26","content":"<p>Ancient Native American and Australian Aboriginal cultures described the Pleiades, which was referred to in Greek mythology as the Seven Sisters, as having seven stars.</p>"},{"id":"32894d46-6a1a-413f-b1e1-2f62f508c431","content":"<p>Although once referred to as the Seven Sisters, the Pleiades appears to have only six stars today.</p>"},{"id":"6b373eb3-8542-4816-a6d7-1b48c14d4bc8","content":"<p>In the time since ancient cultures described the Pleiades as having seven stars, two of the cluster&rsquo;s stars have moved so close together that they now appear as one.</p>"},{"id":"ef016a7b-79fa-4947-b0e9-7323cda88b25","content":"<p>The Pleiades has seven stars, but two are so close together that they appear to be a single star.</p>"}],"keys":["6b373eb3-8542-4816-a6d7-1b48c14d4bc8"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence specifies the reason the Pleiades&rsquo; appearance changed, noting that two of the cluster&rsquo;s stars have moved so close together that they now appear as one star. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence specifies how ancient Native American and Australian Aboriginal cultures described the Pleiades; it doesn&rsquo;t specify the reason the Pleiades&rsquo; appearance changed.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes the appearance of the Pleiades today; it doesn&rsquo;t specify the reason the Pleiades&rsquo; appearance changed. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence explains why two of the Pleiades&rsquo; stars appear to be a single star; it doesn&rsquo;t specify the reason the Pleiades&rsquo; appearance changed. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-784-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c1f2d91d-3af7-4929-aa28-c543c7056143","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959353,"updateDate":1691007959353}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959353, 1691007959353),
    ($SATQ$a965c6ed$SATQ$, $SATQ$dd3a2765-6d7e-4fe3-8c96-e7f4418482ad$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A turtle shell appears external to the animal, protecting its body like armor. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the shell is often incorrectly assumed to be an exoskeleton, a rigid outer casing like that of a crustacean or an insect, when in fact it is an endoskeleton, a part of the turtle&rsquo;s internal bone structure, more akin to a spine or a pair of ribs.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"132a3397-c1bf-4259-9f63-b5f6681532ce","content":"<p>That being said,</p>"},{"id":"6bd3d6d2-15df-449d-aca7-fd075a154ae6","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"61721487-5b03-4e40-9e2f-c456b2d6aea7","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"},{"id":"612bc1cf-b6c2-4e6e-908a-4a9bdfa80dd7","content":"<p>Hence,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["612bc1cf-b6c2-4e6e-908a-4a9bdfa80dd7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "Hence" logically signals that the information in this sentence about turtle shells&mdash;that people incorrectly assume they are exoskeletons&mdash;is a consequence of the shells appearing external to the animal.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "that being said" illogically signals that this sentence qualifies or contrasts with the previous information about turtle shells appearing external to the animal. Instead, it presents a consequence of that information. Choice B is incorrect because "however" illogically signals that this sentence contrasts with the previous information about turtle shells appearing external to the animal. Instead, it presents a consequence of that information. Choice C is incorrect because "for instance" illogically signals that this sentence provides an example supporting the previous information about turtle shells appearing external to the animal. Instead, it presents a consequence of that information.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a965c6ed","external_id":"dd3a2765-6d7e-4fe3-8c96-e7f4418482ad","vaultid":null,"uId":"2155a1a2-6d5d-424e-b8e0-4adec45e8746","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A turtle shell appears external to the animal, protecting its body like armor. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the shell is often incorrectly assumed to be an exoskeleton, a rigid outer casing like that of a crustacean or an insect, when in fact it is an endoskeleton, a part of the turtle&rsquo;s internal bone structure, more akin to a spine or a pair of ribs.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"132a3397-c1bf-4259-9f63-b5f6681532ce","content":"<p>That being said,</p>"},{"id":"6bd3d6d2-15df-449d-aca7-fd075a154ae6","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"61721487-5b03-4e40-9e2f-c456b2d6aea7","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"},{"id":"612bc1cf-b6c2-4e6e-908a-4a9bdfa80dd7","content":"<p>Hence,</p>"}],"keys":["612bc1cf-b6c2-4e6e-908a-4a9bdfa80dd7"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"Hence\" logically signals that the information in this sentence about turtle shells&mdash;that people incorrectly assume they are exoskeletons&mdash;is a consequence of the shells appearing external to the animal.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"that being said\" illogically signals that this sentence qualifies or contrasts with the previous information about turtle shells appearing external to the animal. Instead, it presents a consequence of that information. Choice B is incorrect because \"however\" illogically signals that this sentence contrasts with the previous information about turtle shells appearing external to the animal. Instead, it presents a consequence of that information. Choice C is incorrect because \"for instance\" illogically signals that this sentence provides an example supporting the previous information about turtle shells appearing external to the animal. Instead, it presents a consequence of that information.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323043,"updateDate":1730147323043}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323043, 1730147323043),
    ($SATQ$aa7e10d0$SATQ$, $SATQ$e0d30af8-192d-4f30-8d38-065dbcc99630$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$fd7fd03c-cd73-4ac6-ad7c-6123ec994024$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Species belonging to the Orchidaceae (orchid) family can be found in both tropical and temperate environments.</li>
<li>Orchidaceae species diversity has not been well studied in temperate forests, such as those in Oaxaca, Mexico.</li>
<li>Arelee Estefan&iacute;a Mu&ntilde;oz-Hern&aacute;ndez led a study to determine how many different Orchidaceae species are present in the forests of Oaxaca.</li>
<li>Mu&ntilde;oz-Hern&aacute;ndez and her team collected orchids each month for a year at a site in Oaxaca.</li>
<li>Seventy-four Orchidaceae species were present at the site.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to present the study and its findings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b1793096-4106-4b25-907a-dc4227634133","content":"<p>A study led by Arelee Estefan&iacute;a Mu&ntilde;oz-Hern&aacute;ndez identified a total of 74 Orchidaceae species in the temperate forests of Oaxaca, Mexico.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"f695c1a9-68b8-4f70-a982-de6af154f865","content":"<p>There are orchids in many environments, but there are 74 Orchidaceae species in Oaxaca, Mexico.</p>"},{"id":"ba9d0123-ed9a-433f-9948-65a1148aff3b","content":"<p>Oaxaca, Mexico, is home to temperate forests containing 74 Orchidaceae species.</p>"},{"id":"9feeec78-775c-40af-b5a2-c5badc4e309d","content":"<p>Arelee Estefan&iacute;a Mu&ntilde;oz-Hern&aacute;ndez and her team wanted to know how many different Orchidaceae species are present in the forests of Oaxaca, Mexico, so they conducted a study to collect orchids.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b1793096-4106-4b25-907a-dc4227634133"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. This choice most effectively presents the study and its findings. It opens with the study and names its lead researcher, then tells us its finding: that they identified 74 Orchidaceae species in the temperate forests of Oaxaca, Mexico.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t include the study&rsquo;s findings, so it fails to achieve the goal. It doesn&rsquo;t mention that there was a study at all. &nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t present the study, so it fails to achieve the goal. It doesn&rsquo;t mention that there was a study at all. &nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t include the study&rsquo;s findings, so it fails to achieve the goal. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-719-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$f5dbb474-f162-4104-93f1-1d687140129a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"aa7e10d0","external_id":"e0d30af8-192d-4f30-8d38-065dbcc99630","vaultid":"fd7fd03c-cd73-4ac6-ad7c-6123ec994024","uId":"09175481-911a-491e-a983-79cfae255608","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Species belonging to the Orchidaceae (orchid) family can be found in both tropical and temperate environments.</li>\n<li>Orchidaceae species diversity has not been well studied in temperate forests, such as those in Oaxaca, Mexico.</li>\n<li>Arelee Estefan&iacute;a Mu&ntilde;oz-Hern&aacute;ndez led a study to determine how many different Orchidaceae species are present in the forests of Oaxaca.</li>\n<li>Mu&ntilde;oz-Hern&aacute;ndez and her team collected orchids each month for a year at a site in Oaxaca.</li>\n<li>Seventy-four Orchidaceae species were present at the site.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to present the study and its findings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b1793096-4106-4b25-907a-dc4227634133","content":"<p>A study led by Arelee Estefan&iacute;a Mu&ntilde;oz-Hern&aacute;ndez identified a total of 74 Orchidaceae species in the temperate forests of Oaxaca, Mexico.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"f695c1a9-68b8-4f70-a982-de6af154f865","content":"<p>There are orchids in many environments, but there are 74 Orchidaceae species in Oaxaca, Mexico.</p>"},{"id":"ba9d0123-ed9a-433f-9948-65a1148aff3b","content":"<p>Oaxaca, Mexico, is home to temperate forests containing 74 Orchidaceae species.</p>"},{"id":"9feeec78-775c-40af-b5a2-c5badc4e309d","content":"<p>Arelee Estefan&iacute;a Mu&ntilde;oz-Hern&aacute;ndez and her team wanted to know how many different Orchidaceae species are present in the forests of Oaxaca, Mexico, so they conducted a study to collect orchids.</p>"}],"keys":["b1793096-4106-4b25-907a-dc4227634133"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. This choice most effectively presents the study and its findings. It opens with the study and names its lead researcher, then tells us its finding: that they identified 74 Orchidaceae species in the temperate forests of Oaxaca, Mexico.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t include the study&rsquo;s findings, so it fails to achieve the goal. It doesn&rsquo;t mention that there was a study at all. &nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t present the study, so it fails to achieve the goal. It doesn&rsquo;t mention that there was a study at all. &nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t include the study&rsquo;s findings, so it fails to achieve the goal. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-719-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"f5dbb474-f162-4104-93f1-1d687140129a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959348,"updateDate":1691007959348}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959348, 1691007959348),
    ($SATQ$ab1f424a$SATQ$, $SATQ$138f38d9-26dd-40bc-b574-aea013f78b27$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Working together with the Navajo Nation Department of Water Resources, Dr. Lani Tsinnajinnie analyzed data about snowpack levels in the Chuska Mountains. She found that the snowpack (the amount of snow on the ground) was deepest in early March at lower elevations. At higher elevations, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the snowpack was deepest in mid-March.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"221207cf-f783-460f-8e55-ed86165f2f64","content":"<p>in other words,</p>"},{"id":"d9f4e336-8d7a-411a-aa72-b139ad550073","content":"<p>for instance,</p>"},{"id":"1a83f721-8835-4a7f-815d-7ca6b4c3b112","content":"<p>on the other hand,</p>"},{"id":"c040648f-1529-4161-befa-489747b47829","content":"<p>in summary,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1a83f721-8835-4a7f-815d-7ca6b4c3b112"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. "On the other hand" logically signals that the information in the sentence&mdash;that the snowpack at higher elevations in the Chuska Mountains was deepest in mid-March&mdash;contrasts with the previous information about the snowpack at lower elevations being deepest in early March.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "in other words" illogically signals that information in the sentence is merely a paraphrase or restatement of the previous information about the snowpack at lower elevations. Instead, the information about the snowpack at higher elevations contrasts with that information. Choice B is incorrect because "for instance" illogically signals that the information in the sentence exemplifies the previous information about the snowpack at lower elevations. Instead, the information about the snowpack at higher elevations contrasts with that information. Choice D is incorrect because "in summary" illogically signals that the information in the sentence summarizes the previous information about the snowpack at lower elevations. Instead, the information about the snowpack at higher elevations contrasts with that information.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ab1f424a","external_id":"138f38d9-26dd-40bc-b574-aea013f78b27","vaultid":null,"uId":"bee27c55-146b-4b10-9d01-d15ee4a52de1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Working together with the Navajo Nation Department of Water Resources, Dr. Lani Tsinnajinnie analyzed data about snowpack levels in the Chuska Mountains. She found that the snowpack (the amount of snow on the ground) was deepest in early March at lower elevations. At higher elevations, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the snowpack was deepest in mid-March.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"221207cf-f783-460f-8e55-ed86165f2f64","content":"<p>in other words,</p>"},{"id":"d9f4e336-8d7a-411a-aa72-b139ad550073","content":"<p>for instance,</p>"},{"id":"1a83f721-8835-4a7f-815d-7ca6b4c3b112","content":"<p>on the other hand,</p>"},{"id":"c040648f-1529-4161-befa-489747b47829","content":"<p>in summary,</p>"}],"keys":["1a83f721-8835-4a7f-815d-7ca6b4c3b112"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. \"On the other hand\" logically signals that the information in the sentence&mdash;that the snowpack at higher elevations in the Chuska Mountains was deepest in mid-March&mdash;contrasts with the previous information about the snowpack at lower elevations being deepest in early March.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"in other words\" illogically signals that information in the sentence is merely a paraphrase or restatement of the previous information about the snowpack at lower elevations. Instead, the information about the snowpack at higher elevations contrasts with that information. Choice B is incorrect because \"for instance\" illogically signals that the information in the sentence exemplifies the previous information about the snowpack at lower elevations. Instead, the information about the snowpack at higher elevations contrasts with that information. Choice D is incorrect because \"in summary\" illogically signals that the information in the sentence summarizes the previous information about the snowpack at lower elevations. Instead, the information about the snowpack at higher elevations contrasts with that information.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323042,"updateDate":1730147323042}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323042, 1730147323042),
    ($SATQ$ac8eb085$SATQ$, $SATQ$a37696c4-df78-438e-992f-3921c00cc0ec$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2fcd3924-7b06-4918-a28d-2cff1a026848$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>&ldquo;O2 Arena,&rdquo; an award-winning science fiction story by Nigerian author Oghenechovwe Donald Ekpeki, takes place in an alternate version of Nigeria where breathable air is a rare commodity that is owned and sold by companies. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> people must purchase it with currency called O2 credits.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"39bce539-c4cf-41a3-a8bf-4de12285da13","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"4f6821f2-6783-4d0e-a852-a89c0f818e5a","content":"<p>In any case,</p>"},{"id":"fc2ed9cf-c306-44df-af76-07802721f673","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"28cba137-9336-4973-a81a-389dbe98caba","content":"<p>Earlier,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["39bce539-c4cf-41a3-a8bf-4de12285da13"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The second sentence describes a consequence of the system laid out in the first sentence: because air is owned and sold by companies in this world, people have to buy it. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that means &ldquo;no matter what happens&rdquo; or &ldquo;whatever the situation is,&rdquo; which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. There&rsquo;s only one situation described in the text: a fictional world in which companies own all the breathable air, forcing people to buy it. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But this sentence doesn&rsquo;t disagree with the previous sentence. They both describe the same fictional situation. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates a shift back in time, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. Both sentences use the present tense, as they&rsquo;re describing the same fictional time period. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TRA-E004$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$795ad52e-de74-48b9-8f98-142da5da77c7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ac8eb085","external_id":"a37696c4-df78-438e-992f-3921c00cc0ec","vaultid":"2fcd3924-7b06-4918-a28d-2cff1a026848","uId":"c9872925-f78c-4c90-aa93-bd80aeab56e5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>&ldquo;O2 Arena,&rdquo; an award-winning science fiction story by Nigerian author Oghenechovwe Donald Ekpeki, takes place in an alternate version of Nigeria where breathable air is a rare commodity that is owned and sold by companies. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> people must purchase it with currency called O2 credits.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"39bce539-c4cf-41a3-a8bf-4de12285da13","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"4f6821f2-6783-4d0e-a852-a89c0f818e5a","content":"<p>In any case,</p>"},{"id":"fc2ed9cf-c306-44df-af76-07802721f673","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"28cba137-9336-4973-a81a-389dbe98caba","content":"<p>Earlier,</p>"}],"keys":["39bce539-c4cf-41a3-a8bf-4de12285da13"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The second sentence describes a consequence of the system laid out in the first sentence: because air is owned and sold by companies in this world, people have to buy it. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that means &ldquo;no matter what happens&rdquo; or &ldquo;whatever the situation is,&rdquo; which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. There&rsquo;s only one situation described in the text: a fictional world in which companies own all the breathable air, forcing people to buy it. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But this sentence doesn&rsquo;t disagree with the previous sentence. They both describe the same fictional situation. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates a shift back in time, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. Both sentences use the present tense, as they&rsquo;re describing the same fictional time period. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TRA-E004","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"795ad52e-de74-48b9-8f98-142da5da77c7","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959372,"updateDate":1691007959372}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959372, 1691007959372),
    ($SATQ$ad729337$SATQ$, $SATQ$395ba0d7-8eca-4940-b2a1-bb99f39b6343$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5e08e55b-8eed-433e-95fa-b687bc240f7e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>With its clich&eacute;d imagery of suburban lawns and power lines, John Ashbery&rsquo;s 2004 poem &ldquo;Ignorance of the Law Is No Excuse&rdquo; may seem barren terrain for critical analysis. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> cultural critic Lauren Berlant finds fertile ground in just its first two stanzas, devoting most of a book chapter to deciphering the &ldquo;weight of the default space&rdquo; Ashbery creates in this poem.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"266b910d-872f-4d43-a3e2-94089a876c0b","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"},{"id":"a953a661-bf90-4322-a65e-9ca3eebfb023","content":"<p>Nonetheless,</p>"},{"id":"0e3dd963-9ee9-4d92-989f-bb3e06db29c7","content":"<p>In turn,</p>"},{"id":"d8115b75-fdb8-4648-8a27-284893966f4d","content":"<p>That is,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a953a661-bf90-4322-a65e-9ca3eebfb023"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Nonetheless&rdquo; is a transition that indicates disagreement. The first sentence describes the unlikelihood of finding much for critical analysis in Ashbery&rsquo;s poem (&ldquo;barren terrain&rdquo;), while the second sentence describes how Berlant did in fact find much to analyze in Ashbery&rsquo;s poem (&ldquo;fertile ground&rdquo;), so the transition &ldquo;nonetheless&rdquo; fits perfectly. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates the addition of a new but similar idea, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. The idea in this sentence directly contradicts the idea in the previous sentence. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a cause-and-effect transition, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense in this context&mdash;a poem seemingly having little opportunity for critical analysis would not cause someone to write an extensive critical analysis (in fact, we might expect the opposite). Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates a restatement of the same idea in other words. But the text isn&rsquo;t restating the first idea here. Instead, it&rsquo;s offering a contradiction to the idea expressed in the first sentence. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TRA-H006$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7ec615e1-2602-4a16-ad75-cbd3db312c89$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ad729337","external_id":"395ba0d7-8eca-4940-b2a1-bb99f39b6343","vaultid":"5e08e55b-8eed-433e-95fa-b687bc240f7e","uId":"ae3e61cf-a144-47cc-bb83-bbc0247d8998","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>With its clich&eacute;d imagery of suburban lawns and power lines, John Ashbery&rsquo;s 2004 poem &ldquo;Ignorance of the Law Is No Excuse&rdquo; may seem barren terrain for critical analysis. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> cultural critic Lauren Berlant finds fertile ground in just its first two stanzas, devoting most of a book chapter to deciphering the &ldquo;weight of the default space&rdquo; Ashbery creates in this poem.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"266b910d-872f-4d43-a3e2-94089a876c0b","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"},{"id":"a953a661-bf90-4322-a65e-9ca3eebfb023","content":"<p>Nonetheless,</p>"},{"id":"0e3dd963-9ee9-4d92-989f-bb3e06db29c7","content":"<p>In turn,</p>"},{"id":"d8115b75-fdb8-4648-8a27-284893966f4d","content":"<p>That is,</p>"}],"keys":["a953a661-bf90-4322-a65e-9ca3eebfb023"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;Nonetheless&rdquo; is a transition that indicates disagreement. The first sentence describes the unlikelihood of finding much for critical analysis in Ashbery&rsquo;s poem (&ldquo;barren terrain&rdquo;), while the second sentence describes how Berlant did in fact find much to analyze in Ashbery&rsquo;s poem (&ldquo;fertile ground&rdquo;), so the transition &ldquo;nonetheless&rdquo; fits perfectly. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates the addition of a new but similar idea, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. The idea in this sentence directly contradicts the idea in the previous sentence. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a cause-and-effect transition, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense in this context&mdash;a poem seemingly having little opportunity for critical analysis would not cause someone to write an extensive critical analysis (in fact, we might expect the opposite). Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates a restatement of the same idea in other words. But the text isn&rsquo;t restating the first idea here. Instead, it&rsquo;s offering a contradiction to the idea expressed in the first sentence. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TRA-H006","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7ec615e1-2602-4a16-ad75-cbd3db312c89","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959373,"updateDate":1691007959373}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959373, 1691007959373),
    ($SATQ$aec8d3e8$SATQ$, $SATQ$251dcf25-44d2-489d-9673-339f0f2577d4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$597427a5-f3dc-47ac-bdba-4b65ec9674bb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Chemical leavening agents cause carbon dioxide to be released within a liquid batter, making the batter rise as it bakes.</li>
<li>Baking soda and baking powder are chemical leavening agents.</li>
<li>Baking soda is pure sodium bicarbonate.</li>
<li>To produce carbon dioxide, baking soda needs to be mixed with liquid and an acidic ingredient such as honey.</li>
<li>Baking powder is a mixture of sodium bicarbonate and an acid.</li>
<li>To produce carbon dioxide, baking powder needs to be mixed with liquid but not with an acidic ingredient.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize a difference between baking soda and baking powder. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e9d1a479-672b-40f9-9ece-eb6a2a696379","content":"<p>To make batters rise, bakers use chemical leavening agents such as baking soda and baking powder.</p>"},{"id":"f64ffafc-12e8-4623-97b6-3a6ccdfc163f","content":"<p>Baking soda and baking powder are chemical leavening agents that, when mixed with other ingredients, cause carbon dioxide to be released within a batter.</p>"},{"id":"08d8ec8e-354c-4c8a-89c4-6217104bd293","content":"<p>Baking soda is pure sodium bicarbonate, and honey is a type of acidic ingredient.</p>"},{"id":"f9880917-5622-46a5-a7ef-3730b8af769c","content":"<p>To produce carbon dioxide within a liquid batter, baking soda needs to be mixed with an acidic ingredient, whereas baking powder does not.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f9880917-5622-46a5-a7ef-3730b8af769c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a difference between baking soda and baking powder, noting that baking soda needs to be mixed with an acidic ingredient to produce carbon dioxide but baking powder doesn&rsquo;t. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence focuses on what bakers use to make batters rise; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a difference between baking soda and baking powder. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence provides a general description of baking soda and baking powder; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a difference between them. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence explains what baking soda and honey are; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a difference between baking soda and baking powder. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-736-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b2733c95-8771-4cbf-b2cb-ade721468f92$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"aec8d3e8","external_id"